  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nSurface cyclogenesis is expected off the lee of the Rockies\ntoday as a positively tilted shortwave slides northeast across\nthe northern Plains. For us, that means winds increase out of \nthe southeast and PWATs settle in around 1.5 to 2 inches. \nOverall, it will be an alright day with highs near 80 and breezy\nwinds, but this does set the stage for storm chances late this \nevening and overnight as a trailing cold front sweeps through.\n\nThere is high confidence that the main front does not move\nthrough until early Tuesday morning, however there is solid \nagreement across guidance that there will be some flavor of pre-\nfrontal activity today. One solution, the HRRR, has been \nsuggesting scattered showers develop this morning across \nsouthern MN. This is reasonable given the WAA regime that will \nset up and we are already seeing activity in northwestern IA as \nof 3AM. We do not expect any showers to bubble in the same way \nthey did yesterday morning though, with forecast soundings \nshowing a wedge of dry air above 500mb. Regardless of any \nshowers this morning, there is also agreement on storms \ndeveloping during the afternoon or early evening timeframe \nacross the Dakotas and moving east through central MN during the\nevening. The overall coverage of these evening storms vary \nconsiderably from model to model, and even run to run of each \nmodel. Our highest PoPs, and therefore highest confidence, \nstretches from Alexandria east towards the Mille Lacs and \nKanabec county area. The nature of those pre-frontal storms will\nlikely play a role in how widespread and intense storms are \novernight along the main boundary. With storms likely being very\nclustered and primarily elevated, a marginal risk for severe \nwind and hail remains in place. Perhaps the bigger concern is \nfor heavy rain and localized flash flooding. With the chance for\n2 or even 3 periods of rain today, some areas could see amounts\non the high end of QPF. As with any convective scenario, it \nreally depends on where those storms travel over and if they \npersist for several hours in a given area. On the opposite end,\nnot all locations may see rain as well, with that low \nconfidence in overall coverage area playing a role.\n\nAny lingering showers and cloud cover will begin to clear out by \nmidday Tuesday, though there may be some redevelopment during \nthe afternoon along the boundary as it stretches across \nsoutheast MN and west-central WI. Outside of this, the forecast\nquiets down for a nice Wednesday and Thursday. Northwest winds \ndo increase on Wednesday, with gusts up to around 25 to 30 mph, \nwhich will advect in that drier and cooler airmass. \n\nLong range guidance continues to trend towards a deep trough  \ndeveloping next weekend, which may bring a series of cold \nfronts Friday through Sunday. The latest NBM PoPs reflect this \nidea, with 10 to 30 percent chances each day. Along with any \nrain chance will likely come a strong push of cooler weather \nagain, potentially dropping our highs next week to below \naverage.",
